Several plugins have to pick one host out of several — the initial connection plugin when it
resolves a reader or custom cluster endpoint, and the failover plugins when they choose a
reader to reconnect to. Which host they pick is decided by a **host selection strategy**,
named by a connection property.

{% if wrapper_id == 'ruby' %}
## Available strategies

`random` is the only strategy built into the {{ wrapper.wrapperName }}. It picks uniformly
at random from the hosts that match the requested role and are currently marked available.

| Strategy | Behaviour | Configuration |
|---|---|---|
| `random` | Selects at random from the available hosts matching the requested role. | none |

It is also the default, so leaving the strategy properties unset gives you random
selection. Naming a strategy that is not registered raises
`Unsupported host selection strategy: '<name>'` rather than silently falling back, so a
typo fails fast.

## Where it applies

| Property | Used by | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `initial_connection_host_selector_strategy` | [Initial Connection Strategy Plugin](using-plugins/initial-connection-strategy), when the endpoint resolves to more than one host | `random` |
| `failover_reader_host_selector_strategy` | [Enhanced Failover](enhanced-failover) and [Global Database Failover](using-plugins/gdb-failover), when reconnecting to a reader | `random` |

## Supplying your own strategy

Because there is only one built-in strategy, the wrapper exposes a registration hook so an
application can add its own. Register a selector once at boot, then name it in either
property above:

```ruby
class LeastLagHostSelector
  # @param hosts [Array<HostInfo>] candidate hosts
  # @param role  [Symbol, nil]     the requested role, or nil for no preference
  # @param props [Hash, nil]       connection properties
  # @return [HostInfo, nil]        the chosen host, or nil when none is eligible
  def select_host(hosts, role, props = nil)
    hosts
      .select { |h| (role.nil? || h.role == role) && h.availability == AwsAdvancedRubyDriverWrapper::Host::HostAvailability::AVAILABLE }
      .min_by { |h| your_lag_metric_for(h) }
  end
end

AwsAdvancedRubyDriverWrapper::Services::HostService.register_host_selector(
  'least_lag', LeastLagHostSelector.new
)
```

```yaml
# config/database.yml
production:
  adapter: aws_postgresql
  # ...
  failover_reader_host_selector_strategy: least_lag
```

Two constraints are worth knowing. A selector is shared by every connection in the process,
so `select_host` must be safe to call from multiple threads. And a built-in name cannot be
replaced — registering `random` raises
`Cannot override default host selection strategy: 'random'`.

:::note
Filtering to available hosts is the selector's own responsibility. The built-in `random`
selector does this, and a custom one should too, or it may hand back a host the wrapper has
already marked unavailable.
:::
{% else %}
## Available strategies

| Strategy | Behaviour | Configuration | Since |
|---|---|---|---|
| `random` | The default. Selects at random from the available hosts. | none | 2.0.0 |
| `leastConnections` | Selects the host with the fewest currently active connections. | none | 2.2.0 |
| `roundRobin` | Takes turns across the available hosts in a cycle. Weight the cycle to send more connections to some hosts. | `roundRobinHostWeightPairs`, `roundRobinDefaultWeight` | 2.2.5 |
| `fastestResponse` | Measures response time per host and caches the fastest. | `responseMeasurementIntervalMs` | 2.3.2 |
| `weightedRandom` | Cumulative weighted-random selection; higher weights are chosen proportionally more often. | `weightedRandomHostWeightPairs` | 2.4.0 |
| `lowestLoad` | Selects the least-loaded host, from CPU and lag. | `lowestLoadCpuWeight`, `lowestLoadLagWeight` | 4.1.0 |
| `lowestLoadByCpu` | `lowestLoad` with CPU-dominant defaults (CPU 100, lag 1). | as `lowestLoad` | 4.2.0 |
| `lowestLoadByLag` | `lowestLoad` with lag-dominant defaults (CPU 1, lag 100). | as `lowestLoad` | 4.2.0 |
| `highestLoad` | Selects the most-loaded host, from CPU and lag. | `highestLoadCpuWeight`, `highestLoadLagWeight` | 4.1.0 |
| `highestLoadByCpu` | `highestLoad` with CPU-dominant defaults (CPU 100, lag 1). | as `highestLoad` | 4.2.0 |
| `highestLoadByLag` | `highestLoad` with lag-dominant defaults (CPU 1, lag 100). | as `highestLoad` | 4.2.0 |
| ~~`highestWeight`~~ | **Deprecated** — prefer `lowestLoad`. Selects the host with the largest `HostSpec.weight`. | none | 2.5.0 |

## Configuration parameters

| Parameter | Applies to | Description | Default |
|---|---|---|---|
| `roundRobinHostWeightPairs` | `roundRobin` | Comma-separated `<host>:<weight>` pairs. The weight is how many connections go to that host per cycle, so `instance-1:1,instance-2:4` sends four connections to `instance-2` for every one to `instance-1`. Each weight must be an integer of 1 or more. | none |
| `roundRobinDefaultWeight` | `roundRobin` | Weight for any host not named in `roundRobinHostWeightPairs` — for instance an instance added to the cluster after weights were set. Must be an integer of 1 or more. | `1` |
| `responseMeasurementIntervalMs` | `fastestResponse` | Interval between response-time measurements per host. | `30000` |
| `weightedRandomHostWeightPairs` | `weightedRandom` | Comma-separated `<host>:<weight>` pairs, weights integers of 1 or more. Overrides the per-host weight. | none |
| `lowestLoadCpuWeight` / `highestLoadCpuWeight` | load strategies | Weight of CPU utilization percent in the load calculation. | `1` |
| `lowestLoadLagWeight` / `highestLoadLagWeight` | load strategies | Weight of lag in milliseconds in the load calculation. | `100` |

Load is computed as `load = lag_ms * lagWeight + cpu_percent * cpuWeight`.

:::caution
Three strategies carry prerequisites that are easy to miss.

- `leastConnections` requires internal connection pools. Setting it without enabling them
  throws rather than degrading.
- `fastestResponse` requires the `fastestResponseStrategy` plugin in your
  `{{ wrapper.pluginListParam }}` list; the property alone does nothing.
- The load strategies rely on CPU and lag metrics, which non-Aurora databases may not
  report. Without them the calculation has nothing to work from.
:::

## Where it applies

These strategies are available to the plugins that choose among several hosts, including
[Enhanced Failover](enhanced-failover), the [Failover Plugin](using-plugins/failover),
[Global Database Failover](using-plugins/gdb-failover), the
[Initial Connection Strategy Plugin](using-plugins/initial-connection-strategy), and the
read/write splitting plugins.
